The constraints match the priorities, and the core decorators keep theirs. A few small things:

1. `AutowireAsDecoratorPass::setOrderConstraints()` always clears `container.decoration_order`, even when the attribute has no `within`/`around`. A tag set by hand on the definition is lost silently. Clear it only when the attribute adds its own constraints?
2. In `DecoratorServicePass::sortByOrderConstraints()`, `$keysById + $aliases` lets an id shadow a class name. With decorator A (id `App\Foo`) and decorator B (class `App\Foo`), `within: App\Foo` only targets A.
3. The error message repeats itself: `Invalid "within"/"around" constraints ...: cycle detected in the "within"/"around" constraints`.

Questions: is it intended that XML and `decorate()` have no counterpart to the new keys? And is the `?int` change of `AsDecorator::$priority` fine in a minor?

